1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
 So, definite loops use the for keyword.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,200
 And the idea of a definite loop is it's going to loop

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:07,680
 through some set of things.

4
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,760
 It might be a set of lines in a file.

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:12,780
 It might be a set of characters in a string.

6
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:15,840
 It might be a set of strings in a list of strings.

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:19,240
 But whatever it is, it's sort of gonna run

8
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:21,800
 a finite number of times, depending on the thing

9
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,400
 that it's looping through.

10
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,280
 And we like this.

11
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:28,240
 And it's an easier way to construct it,

12
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,680
 and we actually don't have to deal

13
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:30,840
 with the iteration variable.

14
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:33,080
 The for loop includes a mechanism

15
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:34,960
 to construct the iteration variable for us.

16
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:38,880
 So it's definite loops iterate through the members of a set.

17
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,640
 So here's a very simple for loop.

18
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:45,980
 And so you see the for keyword.

19
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:48,040
 And n is also a keyword.

20
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,100
 And the iteration variable is something we put right here.

21
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:52,880
 This i is declared.

22
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:55,480
 This i is like an assignment statement.

23
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:58,200
 And i is going to take on successive values.

24
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,760
 So i is going to be five the first time through the loop.

25
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:04,340
 Then i is gonna be four the second time through the loop.

26
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:05,680
 Third, two, one.

27
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:09,820
 So i is gonna be assigned five different times

28
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:11,400
 to five different values.

29
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:14,420
 And then the loop is going to run.

30
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:16,960
 It's gonna run once with five, once with four,

31
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:19,840
 once with three, once with two, and once with one.

32
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:22,760
 And so this block of code, we have contracted,

33
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:26,800
 say, execute it five times with these values of i.

34
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,400
 i is that iteration variable.

35
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:32,900
 i is the thing changing through each iteration of the loop.

36
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:37,720
 And so that's why this prints out five, four, three, two, one.

37
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:39,720
 And then when it's done, it finishes it.

38
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:44,240
 So this is a much more direct syntax for looping five times

39
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:45,920
 and setting an iteration variable.

40
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:50,120
 You kind of all combine it into this one thing, right?

41
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:51,260
 All into one thing.

42
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:53,340
 So it's quite nice.

43
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:55,920
 So you don't have to be going through a list of numbers.

44
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,000
 There's all kinds of things

45
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,160
 that we can iterate through with four.

46
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:01,060
 And by the way, while I'm sitting here,

47
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:04,400
 don't, I named my variable friends,

48
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:05,720
 'cause that's a list of strings,

49
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:08,920
 and friend, which is the iteration variable.

50
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:12,120
 I'm using singular and plural because it helps you read it.

51
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:14,200
 Python doesn't understand singular and plural.

52
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:16,360
 So just because you say friends

53
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:18,000
 doesn't mean Python knows it's a list.

54
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,800
 Python does know it's a list,

55
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,040
 but it doesn't know by the name of the variable I've chosen.

56
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:24,800
 That's your basic mnemonic variable warning.

57
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,160
 These are cool variable names,

58
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:28,800
 but I don't want you to be confused by them.

59
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:30,860
 So you can loop through a variable.

60
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:32,720
 So we're gonna take this list of three strings

61
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:33,860
 and stick it in friends.

62
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:35,960
 And so friend is gonna iterate through that.

63
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:38,720
 So the first time through, friend is gonna be Joseph.

64
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:40,600
 Second time through, it's gonna be Glenn.

65
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:43,000
 Third time through, it's going to be Sal.

66
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,880
 And so that just says run this loop,

67
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:48,560
 run this code, the indented code, three times.

68
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,120
 Each time the variable friend

69
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:55,120
 takes on a successive value that's in the friends array.

70
00:02:55,120 --> 00:03:01,440
 So it says happy birthday, Joseph, Glenn, Sally.

71
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:04,080
 And then we come out of the loop and we print done.

72
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:10,780
 So if we try to draw a picture of what this is really doing,

73
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:16,400
 the for loop is actually doing a whole bunch of stuff

74
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:20,060
 that we would have to do with maybe separate statements

75
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:21,000
 in the while loop.

76
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,400
 First it decides how many times to run the loop.

77
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:27,480
 So it's answering the done question, which way do we go?

78
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:30,080
 And it is also then moving I ahead.

79
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:32,280
 It's managing the iteration variable.

80
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:36,040
 If you go back to the, it's initializing it too.

81
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:37,580
 If you go back to the while loop,

82
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:40,000
 we had n equals zero while n greater than zero,

83
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,580
 n equals n minus one.

84
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:45,100
 So we had like three lines to control the loop

85
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:46,880
 to manage the iteration variable.

86
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:49,160
 But with a for loop, we don't have to do that.

87
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:51,480
 And so that's all taken care of.

88
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:55,080
 And so that basically says the for loop,

89
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,720
 by you using a for loop, are we done?

90
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,100
 No, we have five things to work.

91
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:01,360
 Well, set i to the first one, run it.

92
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:02,920
 We're not done 'cause we got one more.

93
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:05,200
 Set it to the second one, third one, fourth one,

94
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:06,960
 fifth one, and now we're done.

95
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:10,640
 And that is all handled in a single line of code

96
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:12,480
 and that includes the iteration variable

97
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:14,620
 and the set of things through which

98
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:16,460
 we are going to iterate through.

99
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:21,780
 I really like the word in.

100
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:24,600
 It is mathematically, I mean,

101
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:28,520
 it reminds me of the set theory where you say

102
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:33,520
 this is a member of this set or the for each.

103
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:35,560
 Math isn't important here, but if you do know math,

104
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:38,160
 the vertical bar means such that, right,

105
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:41,000
 as a member of this set, that kind of stuff,

106
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,020
 member of this set, I'll erase the math stuff

107
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:45,400
 so we don't over math.

108
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,940
 But it's like for each of the values in the set

109
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:52,000
 five, four, three, two, one, run this loop,

110
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,200
 setting the iteration variable i

111
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:55,400
 to the members of that set.

112
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:59,400
 So n reminds me, for those of us who are math-oriented,

113
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:04,400
 n reminds me of a really nice concept in mathematics, okay?

114
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:11,060
 Now, you could think of this as sort of this

115
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:13,640
 looping structure where the for loop,

116
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:15,360
 and this is pretty much how it actually runs

117
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:16,900
 inside the computer, right?

118
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:19,920
 Where it initializes it i, it runs this,

119
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:22,960
 runs this thing five times, and then executes.

120
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:24,120
 That's one way to think about it,

121
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:27,500
 but you could also think about it in a somewhat

122
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:32,080
 more abstract way and think of it as all we're really doing

123
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:35,840
 is we have a contract with Python that says

124
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,400
 we're supposed to run this code five times,

125
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:41,180
 and i's supposed to be five, four, three, two, and one.

126
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:43,600
 So you could imagine this might be what's going on.

127
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,040
 The for loop sets i to five, runs our code.

128
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:48,680
 The for loop sets i to four, runs our code.

129
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:50,960
 The for loop sets i to three, runs our code.

130
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:53,500
 The for loop sets i to two, runs our code.

131
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:55,860
 For loop sets i to one, and runs our code.

132
00:05:55,860 --> 00:06:00,120
 All we know is our code was ran five times,

133
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:04,800
 and by contract, each successive time,

134
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:06,400
 we're getting a different value for i,

135
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,400
 and the value for i is taken from this set.

136
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:11,440
 And so this is just one way to think about it,

137
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:15,240
 to say to yourself, oh yeah, this is one way

138
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:16,600
 to think about it as it's actually,

139
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:18,400
 and this is how it really works,

140
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,880
 but this is also kind of logically the contract

141
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:23,520
 that Python is making for us.

142
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:26,700
 So up next we're gonna talk about taking this notion

143
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:29,120
 of doing something to a lot of items,

144
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:30,680
 but accomplishing something with that,

145
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:33,160
 and I call these loop idioms.

